---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

const pageTitle = "The Loop — The Bigger Picture";
const description =
  "How algorithms, doomscrolling, and FOMO interact to form a broader ethical problem affecting young adults.";
---

<MainLayout title={pageTitle} description={description}>
  <section class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-4xl flex-col gap-10 px-4 py-12">

    <!-- Intro -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        The bigger picture
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
        <p>
          On their own, algorithms, doomscrolling, and FOMO can appear as separate issues.
          One explains how content is selected, another describes a scrolling habit, and
          the third captures a familiar social feeling.
        </p>

        <p>
          In reality, these elements are deeply connected. Together, they form a
          <strong>self-reinforcing system</strong> — a loop where platform design,
          human psychology, and social pressure interact in ways that are difficult
          for individual users to fully see or control.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Interaction explanation -->
    <article class="max-w-prose space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-200 md:text-lg">
      <p>
        Recommendation algorithms decide what is visible. Doomscrolling keeps attention
        locked on emotionally charged content. FOMO and social comparison shape how users
        interpret what they see — and how they feel about themselves.
      </p>

      <p>
        Over time, these elements stop functioning as isolated features and instead merge
        into a broader ethical problem.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- The loop -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        How the loop forms
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
        <p>
          Algorithms are optimised to maximise engagement. They learn which posts hold
          attention and surface more of them. Content that triggers fear, anxiety,
          outrage, or comparison often performs especially well.
        </p>

        <p>
          Doomscrolling emerges when these systems repeatedly prioritise negative or
          crisis-focused material. Engagement reinforces the system’s predictions,
          increasing the likelihood of similar content appearing again.
        </p>

        <p>
          At the same time, FOMO gives this content emotional weight. Seeing others appear
          informed, productive, or socially active creates pressure to keep checking —
          to stay updated and avoid being left behind.
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-slate-100/90">
          <li>Algorithms prioritise emotionally charged content</li>
          <li>Users engage due to fear, comparison, or uncertainty</li>
          <li>Engagement reinforces recommendations</li>
          <li>Emotional stress increases</li>
          <li>Users return to the feed for reassurance or clarity</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Ethical themes -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        Ethical concerns
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Autonomy and manipulation</h4>
          <p>
            While users feel in control of their feeds, content is continuously filtered,
            ranked, and ordered based on behavioural predictions. Emotional responses that
            keep engagement high are often rewarded.
          </p>
          <p>
            This raises a key ethical question: at what point does personalisation become
            manipulation?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Lack of transparency</h4>
          <p>
            Most users have little insight into why certain posts appear and others do not.
            Algorithms are complex, proprietary, and constantly changing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Without transparency, users cannot meaningfully understand or challenge the
            system shaping their attention.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Mental health concerns</h4>
          <p>
            Doomscrolling increases anxiety. FOMO undermines self-esteem. Late-night
            engagement disrupts sleep. These outcomes are rarely the goal of platforms,
            but they are predictable side effects.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Digital consent</h4>
          <p>
            Consent online is often bundled and uninformed. Users technically agree to
            data collection, but few understand how their behaviour is analysed,
            predicted, and monetised.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Exploitation of psychological vulnerabilities</h4>
          <p>
            Engagement-driven systems tend to reward content that aligns with moments of
            vulnerability — anxiety, insecurity, loneliness, or stress — even when doing
            so worsens user wellbeing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Unequal harm</h4>
          <p>
            Not everyone is affected equally. Young adults, teenagers, and users already
            experiencing mental health challenges are more likely to experience negative
            effects.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those who benefit most from engagement-driven systems are often not the ones
            who bear the greatest cost.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Closing -->
    <article class="max-w-prose space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-200 md:text-lg">
      <p>
        Taken together, these issues suggest that the ethical challenge of social media
        does not lie in one feature or one platform.
      </p>

      <p>
        It lies in the <strong>systemic interaction</strong> between economic incentives,
        algorithmic design, and human psychology.
      </p>

      <p>
        Understanding this bigger picture is the first step toward asking a more important
        question: <em>If this is the loop, how — and where — can it be broken?</em>
      </p>
    </article>

  </section>
</MainLayout>
